{
  "title": "Lists",
  "summary": "The everyday growable sequence.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Lists",
      "body": [
        "A `list` is like C#'s `List<T>`, but it can hold any mix of objects and has literal syntax:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "nums = [3, 1, 4]",
        "nums.append(1)       # [3, 1, 4, 1]     (Add)",
        "nums.insert(0, 9)    # [9, 3, 1, 4, 1]",
        "nums.extend([5, 9])  # add many         (AddRange)",
        "nums.pop()           # removes and returns the last: 9",
        "nums.remove(1)       # removes the first 1",
        "len(nums)            # Count",
        "4 in nums            # Contains",
        "nums[-1]             # last item",
        "```",
        "",
        "Indexing and slicing work exactly as on strings, but lists are **mutable**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "items = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]",
        "items.append(\"d\")",
        "items.insert(1, \"x\")",
        "last = items.pop()",
        "print(items, last, len(items))"
      ],
      "answer": "['a', 'x', 'b', 'c'] d 4",
      "explain": "`insert(1, ...)` puts `x` before index 1. `pop()` removes and returns the last item, `d`."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Slices on lists, and aliasing",
      "body": [
        "A slice makes a **new** list, so `copy = items[:]` is a shallow copy. Assigning a list does **not** copy it; both names point at the same object, just like C# references:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "a = [1, 2, 3]",
        "b = a          # same list",
        "c = a[:]       # a copy  (or list(a), or a.copy())",
        "a.append(4)",
        "print(b)       # [1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "print(c)       # [1, 2, 3]",
        "```",
        "",
        "You can also assign to a slice: `a[1:3] = [\"x\"]` replaces two items with one."
      ],
      "pytrace": {
        "code": [
          "cart = [\"pen\", \"ink\"]",
          "mine = cart",
          "copy = cart[:]",
          "mine.append(\"pad\")",
          "copy.append(\"tape\")",
          "print(cart)",
          "print(copy)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "a = [1, 2, 3]",
        "b = a",
        "c = a[:]",
        "b.append(99)",
        "c[0] = 0",
        "print(a)",
        "print(c)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "[1, 2, 3, 99]",
        "[0, 2, 3]"
      ],
      "explain": "`b` is the same list as `a`, so appending through `b` changes `a`. `c` is a separate copy."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Which makes `grid` a 3x3 grid of zeros where changing one row does not change the others?",
      "options": [
        "`grid = [[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]`",
        "`grid = [[0] * 3] * 3`",
        "`grid = [0] * 9`",
        "`grid = [[0, 0, 0]] * 3`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "`[[0] * 3] * 3` makes **three references to the same inner list**, a classic bug. The comprehension (next lesson) builds a fresh row each time."
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each list method to its C# `List<T>` counterpart.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`append(x)`",
          "`Add(x)`"
        ],
        [
          "`extend(xs)`",
          "`AddRange(xs)`"
        ],
        [
          "`insert(i, x)`",
          "`Insert(i, x)`"
        ],
        [
          "`x in items`",
          "`Contains(x)`"
        ],
        [
          "`items.index(x)`",
          "`IndexOf(x)`"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Keep only the last three readings, newest first.",
      "code": [
        "readings = [5, 8, 2, 9, 4]",
        "recent = readings[__1__:]",
        "recent.__2__()",
        "print(recent)  # [4, 9, 2]"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "-3",
        "reverse"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "3",
        "sort",
        "-2",
        "pop"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Shopping list",
      "prompt": [
        "Process commands until `done`, then print the list as `list: a, b, c` (or `list: empty`):",
        "",
        "- `add X` appends X (if it is not already there)",
        "- `remove X` removes X, or prints `no X` if it is missing",
        "- `first X` moves X to the front (adding it if needed)"
      ],
      "starter": "items = []\n",
      "solution": [
        "items = []",
        "while (line := input()) != \"done\":",
        "    cmd, name = line.split(maxsplit=1)",
        "    if cmd == \"add\":",
        "        if name not in items:",
        "            items.append(name)",
        "    elif cmd == \"remove\":",
        "        if name in items:",
        "            items.remove(name)",
        "        else:",
        "            print(f\"no {name}\")",
        "    elif cmd == \"first\":",
        "        if name in items:",
        "            items.remove(name)",
        "        items.insert(0, name)",
        "print(\"list: \" + (\", \".join(items) if items else \"empty\"))"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "adds and removes",
          "stdin": "add milk\nadd eggs\nadd milk\nremove bread\nfirst eggs\ndone\n",
          "expect": [
            "no bread",
            "list: eggs, milk"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "empty",
          "stdin": "add tea\nremove tea\ndone\n",
          "expect": "list: empty"
        },
        {
          "name": "names with spaces",
          "stdin": "add peanut butter\nfirst jam\ndone\n",
          "expect": "list: jam, peanut butter"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`line.split(maxsplit=1)` splits off only the command, so names may contain spaces.",
        "`not in` is the opposite of `in`."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
